{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Yam Suf.</b> Since Aharon died and war came upon them, they turned back by way of Yam Suf. This is the route they retraced when the decree of the spies was decreed upon them, as it says: ״וּסְעוּ הַמִּדְבָּרָה דֶּרֶךְ יַם סוּף״ (Deuteronomy 1). And here they backtracked seven stages, as it says: ״וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל נָסְעוּ מִבְּאֵרֹת בְּנֵי יַעֲקָן מוֹסֵרָה שָׁם מֵת אַהֲרֹן״ — did he die in Moserah? Did he not die at Hor HaHar? Rather, there they turned back and mourned him and eulogized him as though he were before them. Go and check the stages and you will find them to be seven stages from Moserah to Hor HaHar (Tanchuma)." "<b>To skirt the land of Edom.</b> For he did not let them pass through his land." "<b>And the people's spirit grew short on the way.</b> From the trouble of the journey that had become hard for them — they said: Now we were close to entering the Land and we are turning back; thus our fathers turned back and wandered thirty-eight years until this day. Therefore their spirit grew short from the hardship of the way, וּבְלַעַ״ז אנקרוט\"לור. And it is not possible to say וַתִּקְצַר נֶפֶשׁ הָעָם בַּדָּרֶךְ — 'while he was on the way,' without specifying in what the spirit grew short — for everywhere you find קִצּוּר נֶפֶשׁ in Scripture, it specifies there in what it grew short, as in ״וַתִּקְצַר נַפְשִׁי בָּהֶם״ (Zechariah 11), and as in ״וַתִּקְצַר נַפְשׁוֹ בַּעֲמַל יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Judges 10). And any matter that is hard upon a person — the expression קִצּוּר נֶפֶשׁ applies to it: like a person upon whom trouble comes and his mind is not broad enough to accept that thing, and he has no room within his heart for the anguish to dwell there. And for a bothersome matter, the term גֹּדֶל applies — for it is great and heavy upon a person, as in ״וְגַם נַפְשָׁם בָּחֲלָה בִּי״ (Zechariah 11) — it grew great upon me, ״וְיִגְאֶה כַּשַּׁחַל תְּצוּדֵנִי״ (Job 10). The upshot of the exposition: every expression of קִצּוּר נֶפֶשׁ concerning a thing is an expression that one cannot endure it — that the mind cannot bear it."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {2 "The la'az אנקרוט\"לור is Old French encroteler / angoisser, 'to be distressed' (from Latin ango, 'to straiten, oppress')."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:12:00.150861472Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}